    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Building operations (including HVAC, lighting, and electronics) are responsible for 32% of global carbon emissions. To comply with European Union and Austrian requirements, members of AREC must reduce the carbon footprint of existing buildings in their portfolio to zero by\r\n2040. There are four main methods available to achieve net neutral: increasing energy efficiency, decreasing energy loss, introducing\r\nclean energy, and carbon capture.\r\n\r\nOMNI is a combined local radiator floor tile and wind turbine system retrofit that reduces the energy consumption of a heat pump. The retrofit floor and ceiling tiles utilize existing radiator system to circulate hot water in winter and cool water in summer. The wind turbines harness high average wind speeds in Vienna to power heat pump components. The resulting decreased heated water temperature increases heat pump efficiency and decreases heat loss in plumbing system, and the wind turbine adds clean energy to power the radiator floor tile pump and heat pump compressor/evaporator. Together, these changes reduce the carbon footprint of existing buildings.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Kuo, E., Mariak, L., and Somaratne, G. (2024). OMNI: Preparing for a Zero Carbon Future. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/zn216jb2359. https://doi.org/10.25740/zn216jb2359.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
